{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1222038-constructing-my-first-mage-game-as-st-time-loop-purgatory","id":1222038,"title":"Constructing my first Mage Game as ST - Time Loop Purgatory","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-05-25T08:05:27-07:00","id":1222038,"title":"Constructing my first Mage Game as ST - Time Loop Purgatory","content":"So I've played Mage a few times, and religiously read M20, but I've yet to actually ST Mage! I'm going to be running my first game, likely a one-shot, in a few weeks time.<br /> <br /> The concept I've got is that the PCs, who are all Mages (but don't have to be affiliated with eachother at all), are lured to this town where the game is set. The town is Silent Hill esque. Dark, mysterious, the kind of place you want to leave. But each PC has a reason to come there. Their missing friend was spotted there. They were invited by a person they respect. They have some history with the town that they want to resolve. They saw the place in their dreams. Each of these Mages should have a fundamental personality flaw or character defect, from rage to addiction to bitterness over past failings...<br /> <br /> Upon arrival, these Mages will recognise eachother and realize something is wrong. Professor so-and-so, the Etherite who invited one of the PCs, isn't there. The missing friend who was supposedly spotted in the town isn't recognized by *any* of the NPCs. And as they move through the town, they are confronted by situations which land far too close to home, lining up with the problems in their past.<br /> <br /> Essentially, this town is a trap. If one of the Mages attempts to leave, they'll find a serial escalation of obstacles. A broken bridge. Then their car breaks down. When they start walking, a cramp. Then heavy mist and rain. If they don't get the hint, eventually they get lost and confused and wind up walking back towards the town. The events of the Chronicle play out over three days, with every NPC accounted for with a specific sort of planned activity across the time and space of the setting. This is subject to interference of course... the Sheriff might plan to have dinner with the woman he's having an affair with, but if a cyborg blows up a few houses thanks to the PC's direct or indirect involvement naturally his plans will be cancelled.<br /> <br /> At the end of the three days, things play out again, thanks to some bizarre use of Time. Each of the NPCs go about their planned activity, all over again. Only the PCs (and other Mages perhaps?) seem to have retained their memories from the 3 days resetting. If a PC had died, then they'll return alive, only remembering up the fatal moment. This means that as the story replays, the PCs can discover secrets about the town and get to the bottom of things, learning how to exploit known events to get certain things to happen.<br /> <br /> The events are happening as they are because of an Archmage Nephandus (perhaps even hidden among them), who has chosen some Mages they think are ripe for conversion. The whole point of the exercise is to gradually wear away their will; physical injuries are healed when time resets, but loss of temporary or permanent Willpower is not. Derangements gained, or brought to the fore, will remain. And each of these Mages is fragile in some sense, that's why they were chosen for this. As they repeat this 3 day cycle, over and over, the Nephandus will seek to corrupt them, simply going over and over until they get an ending THEY like.<br /> <br /> For example, a Shamanistic type with anger issues (perhaps from a Totem association) might simply go Ultra-Violence one time around. Their Stress Atavism comes to the fore, and they slaughter the town to a man. And then the time loop just... stops. Time actually moves forward this time around, and they are robbed of the reversal which (in their mind) made what they were doing amoral and somewhat moot. Or perhaps when the grieving masochist finally succumbs and decides to kill themselves, that will be the version of time which sticks. But no matter how bad the outcome, the Nephandus can always make them an offer...<br /> <br /> &quot;I can let you go around one more time, bring back your poor friend, all of those poor people, but in exchange you have to do a small favour for me...&quot;<br /> <br /> The only way to reset everything is to go to the Cauls, with their guidance. Ideally I'll only pull this card once the situation is UTTERLY ruined and the remaining PCs are in a position where it seems like an attractive solution. And the most bitter part of the ending, if they *do* choose to succumb, would be that once they've been to the Cauls, they have no interest in reversing what went wrong anymore.<br /> <br /> As for victory conditions... I can't think of any. Essentially I'm at a loss for really subtle means of corruption which would actually drive the PCs towards losing themselves, and ways that the PCs could fight back against the situation they've been placed in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-26T02:31:02-07:00","id":1222191,"title":"","content":"So Majora's Mask meets Silent Hill with a little bit of Killing Joke thrown in for good measure.<br /> <br /> It's a really compelling setting, and you could do quite a lot with it, though if it's a one-shot you might want to be careful about how grand the scope becomes. I can imagine a campaign like this easily running for at least 6 weeks with each session restarting the time loop.<br /> <br /> You might want to think about how Corr 3 or Spirit 3 mages might be blocked off from either teleporting away of jumping off into the spirit world. You could just put up a huge block, but it could be more interesting for their powers to work but less good. Teleporting may only take them to a random or specific location in the town, while stepping sideways might confine them to a darkened nexus version of the town. Alternatively, they might already be in the spirit world and so stepping sideways doesn't work at all, and that fact is actually a clue that they may or may not pick up on.<br /> <br /> The only major problem I have this this set up is the endgame. I get what you're trying to do, but forcing the mages to screw themselves over and then promise them to unscrew themselves in exchange for their souls isn't very nephandi in my opinion, it's certainly something a demon would do, but the nephandi are not interested in &quot;tricking&quot; people into joining them, but having them willing to join. Sure, they'll trick people into carrying out their schemes, but recruitment must be desired by their goal. While demons just want control, the nephandi want to have their victims realise the truth that all of reality is nothing but a horrible disgusting mess, and that its destruction is the only thing worth fighting for.<br /> <br /> If I was running that setting, I would definitely play up the &quot;time keeps looping therefore there are no consequences&quot; side of things. The Nephandus would want to let the mages understand just how good it feels to be powerful and to use that power to act out against an inherently oppressive construct such as a time loop. Get the mages really comfortable with the idea of destruction, perhaps even get them to love it. Let aspects of their real lives enter the village, people they know or situations they have experienced, anything that means something to them, and allow them to destroy it irreversibly in an act of utter catharsis under the guise that everything will just reset anyway.<br /> <br /> And then it doesn't. They wake up the next morning and realise that their most destructive day is the day that sticks, and the question then becomes, do they care anymore? Have they grown so comfortable with their destructive impulses, so in love with the power they felt, that they don't care about the damage they have caused? If no, then they are absolutely ripe for the picking. The nephandi may reveal themselves and explain how they have &quot;set them free&quot; from the social constraints which deny their true potential, and allow them to understand why reality and all that is keeping us in place needs to burn.<br /> <br /> If they are ready to pass through the cauls, they will be doing so as eager warriors of descent. Otherwise, the nephandi may simply leave them as broken shells of their former selves. Perhaps they may give the promise of reseting things from them, but perhaps only one of them will get the full redemption, and the mages will have to fight or decide amongst themselves to see which one of them gets to have the things they destroyed returned, and the trauma returned. Then the naphandi will leave, satisfied that in some small way that they have done their work. The mages return to the world broken and perhaps even resentful, while the nephandus simply resets their world and prepares to start again with a new group.<br /> <br /> As for what the mage's can do. I'd say that all the &quot;obvious&quot; leads that you give them should always be things that help this nephandus's goals. If the mage wants to find another way out, they have to investigate it for themselves. To reiterate, any &quot;clues&quot; you just tell them about will be things the nephandus wants them to find. But if the mages go off the beaten track and start investigating things by themselves, they'll start to find real clues towards the reality of this world. Perhaps a time mage realises that this is not just a temporal anomaly, perhaps a prime mage may work out where the magic is coming from. Maybe they need to work out how to get somewhere like the bottom of a hotel or the top of a clock tower, or somewhere even less conspicuous, as this is where the nephandus' tools which keep the effect functioning are. However, any time they get a little closer, something stops them, and it will be up to the mage to use the time loops to figure out a way around the obstacle to get ever closer to the source of the magic.<br /> <br /> If the mage discovers what is going on, then they will be attacked and perhaps even &quot;disappear&quot; from the time loop entirely. They could even be in someways incorporated into the nephandis designed conditioning. It's up to you what you'll have that player doing for the remainder of the session. Needless to say, it might be best to take them out of the room and speak to them secretly about what they've discovered and how the nephandus reacts to it. But again, that's all up to you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-26T06:20:24-07:00","id":1222228,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12193_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12193\" data-vbnamecard=\"12193\">Saikou</a> I was going for Silent Hill meets Majora's Mask meets Groundhog day in terms of themes and mechanics ;-) But I can see a similarity with the Killing Joke now!<br /> <br /> I was thinking that Correspondence 3 wouldn't be able to get you out of the area, or pull anything in. Instead it lets you teleport quite easily within the town.<br /> <br /> Spirit 3 won't let you step sideways, however many successes with Spirit would allow them to understand that the town itself is drenched in a shallow point in the Umbra... a sort of crossroads. You could call it a Shallowing. The problem isn't that you can't cross the Gauntlet, it's that there isn't much of a Gauntlet or distinct &quot;Umbra&quot; here to work with, instead it's just an eerie place where the low and high and middle umbras all spill into the world... which might explain the &quot;Pyramid Head&quot; type anomalies (their own pasts and problems shaping the world thanks to the High Umbra, a la Silent Hill), the presence of death and decay (thanks to the low umbra) and a feeling of ineffable mystery and unknowable powers stemming from the natural areas around the town (middle umbra).<br /> <br /> That's a good point with the Nephandic endgame... I was thinking of having them grow distanced from people by the continual repeat, so placing more emphasis on that could be a good idea. The scenario is set up to make them feel better than sleepers, more powerful and aware, to make them seem less important as time goes on. In the end, it's almost like the perfect recipe for making a Nephandus. &quot;Life is meaningless, people mean nothing, the universe was a mistake&quot; is quintessentially Nephandic, and this scenario draws those ideas out of people organically. They just need to get so used to treating the time loop like this that it affects how they see the real world.<br /> <br /> One possible mechanical draw towards darkness can be that Maleficia, and Magick where they are moving towards a more Nephandic Paradigm (Creation was a Mistake, We are not Men, One-Way Ride to Oblivion, Might is Right) in the way they perform it, will always be Coincidental. This place is its own Reality Zone, and it is somewhat permissive with mysticism, but if you're playing along with the whole *point* of this town then you'll find magick easier. Furthermore, thanks to a Mind effect, everyone gets the mechanical benefits of the Monster Nature. The town doesn't make you a monster, but anyone who commits a monstrous act within it finds their mental reserves somewhat restored. A player might realize this and use it if they're running low on Willpower, however someone smarter might feel like this is all another trap... but if I do it right someone might feel like they have to take that chance.<br /> <br /> And I had a new idea... &quot;okay now I'll take you to the Cauls!&quot; is a kind of tacky ending. It's like the newly evil Mage is being taken on an evil vacation. Instead, establish early in the Chronicle a place which symbolically represents the cauls. A forsaken mine which used to employ the town, or a well maybe. Something that's a hole in the ground at least. A PC lowering themselves into the black water would be powerful ending (if things played out that way).<br /> <br /> As for the Nephandi themselves... the Nephandi worship dark gods which are reflections of themselves, extensions of themselves. It would be interesting if this town were the site where a powerful Nephandic Archmage *died*; the one pulling the strings now is an Earthbound &quot;demon&quot; of sorts which is what's left of the Nephandus.<br /> <br /> Now the people in the town should be real people, however they should serve the purpose of the town. They're racked by vice, and show how shitty humankind is if you place them in a shitty place (on this scale, the town, but the *point* is that this applies to a lesser extent to the rest of the world). Every household has a dark secret, all around the town terrible things are happening...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 05-26-2018, 09:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-26T11:09:41-07:00","id":1222282,"title":"","content":"The setting will be the Cornish town of Pytt (no prizes for guessing what Pytt translates to from Cornish). A small mining and fishing town built around Wheal Pytt, which was once a source of copper and tin and great wealth. In time the mining industry died, and the town atrophied. Before the mine closed, there were rumours of a great treasure hidden within the base of the mine, and several miners died bizarre deaths in the following weeks. Tomb of Ramases stuff. Choked on his own tongue. Dropped dead while walking to work. Had a fit in church. This was the last nail in the mine's coffin.<br /> <br /> The chronicle is set in the mid 90s. In the late 80s a descendant of the owner of Wheal Pytt decided to revive it, having been educated at a mining college. He hoped to get some money and experience out of it, along with treasure. While working at a vein of black ore, they found something terrible, and woke up something that had been asleep for a long time.<br /> <br /> Now the Player Characters have been drawn to Pytt. Perhaps they seek the treasure, or have a family connection. Perhaps they were invited by someone worthy of their time, or are looking for a place to recuperate after some tragedy. Maybe they were drawn to it by its strange spiritual energy, or some old pagan site in the hills...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-01T07:12:33-07:00","id":1223902,"title":"","content":"My two PCs thus far are:<br /> <br /> - The Furious Akashic. Grew up in a poor estate in London, truant, anti-social menace, tough kid. Flirted with gang activity and almost ended up in prison, but got pulled off of his downward trajectory when he was forced to take tai chi lessons and carry out community service. There he met his Akashic mentor, who recognized his incredible potential and guided him towards tranquility. This mentor was the only person who had ever seen good or potential in him. The other Akashics look down upon this newcomer, who lacks a pan-asian heritage and any sense of mysticism, but his mentor supports him through all of this... until he is killed, with no trace of the murderer being found. He has come to Pytt because of a vision of a red dragon setting the moorland and fields on fire, around the town, bringing down vengeance. He believes that in this place he will find whoever killed his mentor.<br /> <br /> Weakness: Rage, preference for power and fear over mysticism and tranquility, reliance on violence and threats. His paradigm could be bent towards Descension, if he fails to learn from his dead mentor's example and remain steadfast as the scenario assaults his morality.<br /> <br /> Strength: A sense of honour and integrity, and the belief that really shitty people who seem hopeless can get better and do good.<br /> <br /> - The Mercenary Occultist. This is a woman who always looks out for #1. For her, magick is just a diverse set of &quot;keys&quot; which control reality, left from the beginning of time. And Wonders, Trinkets, Grimoires, Instruments... these are the physical manifestations of keys. So she trades in them. Her magickal style is chaotic; some Hermetic runes, some gutter magick she picked up from a homeless girl, some wicka, some paganism... when she meets other mages, and other cultures, she studies and trades, both ideas and objects. For the last 5 years she has been infamous in London, selling anything she can get her hands on, to anyone, with no questions... for the right price. It's none of her business what this strange Vampire or this Syndicate executive want with something she acquired; if they offer a fair price, she's game. She has come to Pytt to trade, but the person she was meant to be meeting is nowhere to be seen...<br /> <br /> Weakness: Mercurial. She lacks a moral compass and already frames her own benefit at the center of her decision-making.<br /> <br /> Strength: Her appreciation of various traditions and cultures means that she has an appreciation for humanity, even if she isn't the most humane person.<br /> <br /> So, now I'm wondering how a clever Nephandus would manipulate them? The town being populated with shitty people who will drive the PCs to extreme actions is a good start, but I wonder what they could do which would really exploit the PC's flaws...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-01-2018, 10:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-01T12:16:20-07:00","id":1223976,"title":"","content":"The Akashic guy has a really obvious one. In the town another Akashic is there who had a similar vision of a red dragon and is investigating it. However, her refuses to tell the player anything saying it is business only for the Akashics. If player protests that the is indeed an Akashic, then this NPC will scoff and belittle him, refusing to beleive for an instant of his connection to the monks. The player can be made to beleive her can in some way convince this Akashic to aid him in his search, but he will always come up short, and at worst be accused to stealing Akashic secrets.<br /> If they mention their mentor at all, then things might shift a little. This NPC knows about the mentor and will spit that his death was well deserved, that he had lost The Way, and threatened to bring great travesty to the tradition, and his accepting the player as a student was evidence of that loss of sight. He may even say the Player was responsible for his death, tell him that had he not felt sorry for him the master would have still been alive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-01T15:22:12-07:00","id":1224011,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1222038-constructing-my-first-mage-game-as-st-time-loop-purgatory?p=1223976#post1223976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Akashic guy has a really obvious one. In the town another Akashic is there who had a similar vision of a red dragon and is investigating it. However, her refuses to tell the player anything saying it is business only for the Akashics. If player protests that the is indeed an Akashic, then this NPC will scoff and belittle him, refusing to beleive for an instant of his connection to the monks. The player can be made to beleive her can in some way convince this Akashic to aid him in his search, but he will always come up short, and at worst be accused to stealing Akashic secrets.<br /> If they mention their mentor at all, then things might shift a little. This NPC knows about the mentor and will spit that his death was well deserved, that he had lost The Way, and threatened to bring great travesty to the tradition, and his accepting the player as a student was evidence of that loss of sight. He may even say the Player was responsible for his death, tell him that had he not felt sorry for him the master would have still been alive.</div> </div> </div> </div>I really like this idea! I'm wondering whether I should make this Akashic &quot;real&quot; or not... it would make a lot of sense if they were an extension of the Nephandus. They're a foil which is simply intended to infuriate him. A long mystical heritage, has adventured across Asia, master of The Way, sage and self-assured. I'm also thinking that for some somewhat heavy-handed symbolism I could add an encounter with a dog, who has gone feral since his master died.<br /> <br /> With my mercenary occultist, I think it might piss her off to no end if the town is populated with selfish and greedy people who are <i>shortsighted</i>. She's used to people who demonstrate rational self-interest, and try and look out for their own interests. But these small-minded people can't see two steps ahead of themselves, and are just bad at being selfish. I could also add an NPC who acts as a vision of the net result of this PC's behaviour. A lonely rich woman who poisoned every friendship and alliance with her greed, won every game, and ended up with all of the baubles and riches in the world but absolutely nothing of value. The point isn't to teach her that being selfish is wrong, it's to imprint a sense of the utter pointlessness of existence, how even all of this accumulation will still leave you in a state of angst when you stop to catch your breath.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}